[s0l0!-header][h1]The perror and strerror Functions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

When a library function sets errno, a cryptic error number is assigned. For a more descriptive message about the error, you can use [b]perror()[/b]. You can also obtain the message using [b]strerror()[/b] in the [b]string.h[/b] header file, which returns a pointer to the message text.

[b]perror()[/b] must include a string that will precede the actual error message. Typically, there is no need for both perror() and strerror() for the same error, but both are used in the program below for demonstration purposes:[code format="c" codeId="2165"]FILE *fptr;
errno = 0;

fptr = fopen("c:\\nonexistantfile.txt", "r");
if (fptr == NULL) {
[b]  perror("Error");[/b]
  fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", [b]strerror(errno)[/b]);
  exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
} [/code]
There are more than a hundred [b]error codes[/b]. Use these statements to list them:[code format="c" codeId="2166"]for (int x = 0; x < 135; x++)
  fprintf(stderr, "%d: %s\n", x, [b]strerror(x)[/b]); [/code]
