[s0l0!-header][h1]The feof and ferror Functions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

In addition to checking for a NULL file pointer and using errno, the [b]feof()[/b] and [b]ferror()[/b] functions can be used for determining file I/O errors:
[b]feof(fp)[/b]  Returns a nonzero value if the end of stream has been reached, 0 otherwise. feof also sets EOF.
[b]ferror(fp)[/b]  Returns a nonzero value if there is an error, 0 for no error.

The following program incorporates several exception handling techniques:[code format="c" codeId="2168"]FILE *fptr;
int c;

errno = 0;

fptr = fopen("myfile.txt", "r");
if (fptr == NULL) {
  fprintf(stderr, "Error opening file. %s\n", strerror(errno));
  exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

while ((c = getc(fptr)) != EOF) /* read the rest of the file */
  printf("%c", c);

if ([b]ferror(fptr)[/b]) {
  printf("I/O error reading file.");
  exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}
else if ([b]feof(fptr)[/b]) {
  printf("End of file reached.");
} [/code]
Program output will vary. But if the file opens properly and the program completes reading the entire file, then the following message displays: "End of file reached."
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

